        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Who is a neuropsychological assessment for?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">A neuropsychological assessment can be carried out at any age: children, adolescents, adults and older adults. The content, the tasks used and the length all change with age and with the reason for referral. With younger children the process is usually divided across more than one session. The table below shows the most common reasons at each age.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border border-warm-tertiary rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-warm">
                <th class="text-left font-semibold text-ink px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Age group</th>
                <th class="text-left font-semibold text-ink px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Common reason for referral</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-ink-muted">
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Children (0–12)</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Attention difficulties, learning difficulties, suspected developmental delay</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Adolescents (12–18)</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Drop in academic performance, attention and organisation difficulties</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Adults</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Attention and memory complaints, changes following head injury</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Older adults</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Forgetfulness, changes in daily functioning, neurological presentations</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">How does the assessment work?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">The assessment has three steps: an initial meeting, administration and a feedback meeting. How long the administration takes depends on the range of domains being measured, and it is divided across sessions where that is helpful. Breaks are given between tasks, since tiredness affects the result and that matters.</p>
        <ol class="space-y-5">
          <li class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">1. Initial meeting</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We talk about the reason for referral, developmental history and any referral from a school or physician. Here we decide together which question the assessment is meant to answer.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">2. Administration</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Pen-and-paper work, recall tasks and puzzle-like activities are used. These are not exams; they show how a person thinks. Breaks are given throughout.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">3. Making sense of the results</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Results are interpreted for the age group. No single score is read on its own; what matters is the whole profile — which areas are strong and which need support.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2 text-sm">4. Feedback meeting</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We go through the results together, discuss what they mean in daily life and what can be done next. For children this meeting is held with the parents.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">What do you receive at the end?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">The process closes with a <strong class="text-ink font-medium">feedback meeting</strong> in which the results are gone through together. Which areas were assessed, what was observed and what the results mean in daily life are all covered there.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The meeting exists so the numbers do not stand alone. Seeing a result is not the same as understanding what it means.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Does a neuropsychological assessment give a diagnosis?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">A neuropsychological assessment does not give a diagnosis on its own. It maps cognitive functioning, and that map is one part of a diagnostic process rather than the whole of it. In neurological presentations — head injury, epilepsy, situations following a stroke — the assessment is carried out alongside a physician, and the diagnostic decision rests with them.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">We prefer to say this at the outset. Setting the right expectation of an assessment is part of making its result useful.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">What is the difference between assessment and therapy?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">An assessment is aimed at understanding a situation and runs over a limited number of sessions. Therapy is ongoing work. Neither replaces the other, though one can lead to the other. The table sets out the differences.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border border-warm-tertiary rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-warm">
                <th class="text-left font-semibold text-ink px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">&nbsp;</th>
                <th class="text-left font-semibold text-ink px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Neuropsychological assessment</th>
                <th class="text-left font-semibold text-ink px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Therapy</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-ink-muted">
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Purpose</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">To understand a situation and map it</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">To work on something ongoing</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Length</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">A limited number of sessions</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Sessions of 50 minutes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary font-medium text-ink">Outcome</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">A feedback meeting</td><td class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-warm-tertiary">Ongoing work</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-5 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Afterwards</td><td class="px-5 py-3">Therapy, school support or a referral to a physician may be suggested</td><td class="px-5 py-3">The process continues, reviewed together</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mt-8">Therapy may be suggested after an assessment, but it is not required.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">We cover the subject in more detail here: <a href="/en/blog/neuropsychological-assessment.html" class="text-primary underline hover:no-underline">What Is a Neuropsychological Assessment and When Is It Requested?</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
